<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard II, Duke of Normandy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 963–1026</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Richard the Good</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard II, called the Good, was ruler of Normandy from 996 to 1026, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>. He was the first to use the title of duke regularly, put down a peasant revolt early in his reign and promoted monastic reform, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-volpiano">William of Volpiano</a> to Fécamp. His sister Emma married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he was the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-ii-duke-of-normandy.jpg" alt="Richard II, Duke of Normandy" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_the_Good_of_Normandy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Richard II was the eldest surviving son and heir of Richard the Fearless and his wife Gunnor de Crêpon. He succeeded his father as ruler of Normandy in 996. During his minority, the first five years of his reign, his uncle Count Rodulf of Ivry acted as regent, held the power and put down a peasant insurrection at the start of the reign.</p><p>Richard had deep religious interests and found much in common with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-of-france">Robert II of France</a>, whom he helped militarily against the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. He also forged a marriage alliance with Duke Geoffrey I of Brittany, marrying Geoffrey's sister Judith while his own sister Hawise married Geoffrey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> had begun raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> again and sold their plunder in Normandy. Richard gave them sanctuary and welcomed them, which broke a treaty between his father and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred II</a> of England. Æthelred ordered that Richard be captured, bound and brought to England, and sent an attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotentin-peninsula">Cotentin Peninsula</a>. The English were not prepared for the rapid response of the Norman cavalry and were defeated.</p><p>Richard then tried to improve relations by marrying his sister Emma to Æthelred. The marriage later gave his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> the basis of his claim to the English throne. Emma fled to Normandy with her sons Edward and Alfred, and Æthelred followed. After Æthelred died in 1016, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> married Emma, and Richard had to recognise the new regime.</p><p>He kept contact with Scandinavian Vikings throughout his reign and employed Viking mercenaries. After the St Brice's Day Massacre ordered by Æthelred in 1013, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, stopped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> and was well received. Richard concluded an alliance with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Richard continued his father's commission to his clerk and confessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dudo-of-saint-quentin">Dudo of Saint-Quentin</a> to write a history of the dukes of Normandy. Dudo had written records and eyewitnesses to draw on, but his history is more panegyric than chronicle, telling of the pagan conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the legitimacy of the dukes' rule, and should not be read uncritically.</p><p>In 1025 and 1026 Richard confirmed gifts of his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> to the abbey of Saint-Ouen at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. His grants to monasteries indicate where he held ducal control: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, the Éverecin, the Cotentin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pays-de-caux">Pays de Caux</a> and Rouen. He died in 1026 and was succeeded by his eldest son, Richard III.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard II of Normandy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rulers were styled counts or princes at first; <b>Richard II</b> was the first to use the title of duke regularly, early in the eleventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Normandy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Fécamp, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1003" aria-label="Normandy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 450 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Richard II, Duke of Normandy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1003">What was here in 1003? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
